    "create_time": 1773020775.502,
    "update_time": null,
    "content": {
     "content_type": "text",
     "parts": [
      "General rules (not tax advice for your specific case):\n\n- **GRA stipend on a W-2:** wages, taxable as normal. Already reported via the W-2.\n- **Tuition waiver / remission:** for graduate students, a *qualified tuition reduction* for teaching or research assistants is generally excluded from income under IRC §117(d) if it's for the GRA/GTA's own tuition. It usually doesn't show up as income on the W-2.\n- **1098-T:** box 1 = qualified tuition billed/paid, box 5 = scholarships/grants (which may include the waiver). If box 5 ≥ box 1 and the excess is only tuition-related, typically nothing extra is taxable. If box 5 > box 1 (e.g., a fellowship that also covered living costs), the **excess is taxable income** reported as scholarship income.\n- **Education credits:** if your tuition was fully covered by tax-free assistance, you generally can't also claim the American Opportunity / Lifetime Learning Credit on those same expenses.\n\nIn FreeTaxUSA: enter the 1098-T under education; it will compute whether any scholarship is taxable. If box 5 ≤ box 1, expect $0 additional.\n\nWhen in doubt, VT's graduate school or a VITA site can confirm how your waiver is coded."
     ]
    },
